DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       nnaannoosslleeeepp()  suspends  the execution of the calling thread until either
       at least the time specified in _*_d_u_r_a_t_i_o_n has elapsed, or  the  delivery
       of  a  signal  that triggers the invocation of a handler in the calling
       thread or that terminates the process.

       If the call is interrupted by a signal handler, nnaannoosslleeeepp() returns -1,
       sets _e_r_r_n_o to EEIINNTTRR, and writes the remaining time into  the  structure
       pointed  to  by  _r_e_m unless _r_e_m is NULL.  The value of _*_r_e_m can then be
       used to call nnaannoosslleeeepp() again and complete the  specified  pause  (but
       see NOTES).

       The  ttiimmeessppeecc(3)  structure  is  used to specify intervals of time with
       nanosecond precision.

       The value of the nanoseconds field must be in the range [0, 999999999].

       Compared to sslleeeepp(3) and uusslleeeepp(3), nnaannoosslleeeepp() has the  following  ad‐
       vantages:  it provides a higher resolution for specifying the sleep in‐
       terval; POSIX.1 explicitly specifies that it  does  not  interact  with
       signals; and it makes the task of resuming a sleep that has been inter‐
       rupted by a signal handler easier.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On  successfully  sleeping  for the requested duration, nnaannoosslleeeepp() re‐
       turns 0.  If the call is interrupted by a signal handler or  encounters
       an error, then it returns -1, with _e_r_r_n_o set to indicate the error.

