              999999999] or _t_v___s_e_c was negative.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       POSIX.1  specifies  that  nnaannoosslleeeepp()  should  measure time against the
       CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE clock.  However,  Linux  measures  the  time  using  the
       CCLLOOCCKK__MMOONNOOTTOONNIICC  clock.   This  probably  does  not  matter,  since the
       POSIX.1 specification  for  cclloocckk__sseettttiimmee(2)  says  that  discontinuous
       changes in CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE should not affect nnaannoosslleeeepp():

              Setting  the  value  of  the CCLLOOCCKK__RREEAALLTTIIMMEE clock via cclloocckk__sseett‐‐
              ttiimmee(2) shall have no effect on threads that are blocked waiting
              for a relative time service based upon this clock, including the
              nnaannoosslleeeepp() function.  Consequently, these time  services  shall
              expire when the requested duration elapses, independently of the
              new or old value of the clock.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       POSIX.1‐2024.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       POSIX.1‐2001.

       In  order  to  support  applications requiring much more precise pauses
       (e.g., in order to control some  time‐critical  hardware),  nnaannoosslleeeepp()
       would  handle  pauses  of up to 2 milliseconds by busy waiting with mi‐
       crosecond precision when called from a thread scheduled under  a  real‐
       time  policy  like  SSCCHHEEDD__FFIIFFOO or SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR.  This special extension was
       removed in Linux 2.5.39, and is thus not available in Linux  2.6.0  and
       later kernels.

NNOOTTEESS
       If  the _d_u_r_a_t_i_o_n is not an exact multiple of the granularity underlying
       clock (see ttiimmee(7)), then the interval will be rounded up to  the  next
       multiple.  Furthermore, after the sleep completes, there may still be a
       delay  before  the  CPU  becomes free to once again execute the calling
       thread.

       The fact that nnaannoosslleeeepp() sleeps for a relative interval can  be  prob‐
       lematic  if the call is repeatedly restarted after being interrupted by
       signals, since the time between the interruptions and restarts  of  the
       call  will  lead to drift in the time when the sleep finally completes.
       This problem can be avoided by using  cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp(2)  with  an  ab‐
       solute time value.

BBUUGGSS
       If a program that catches signals and uses nnaannoosslleeeepp() receives signals
       at  a very high rate, then scheduling delays and rounding errors in the
       kernel’s calculation of the sleep  interval  and  the  returned  _r_e_m_a_i_n
       value  mean  that  the _r_e_m_a_i_n value may steadily _i_n_c_r_e_a_s_e on successive
       restarts  of  the  nnaannoosslleeeepp()  call.   To  avoid  such  problems,  use
       cclloocckk__nnaannoosslleeeepp(2)  with the TTIIMMEERR__AABBSSTTIIMMEE flag to sleep to an absolute
       deadline.

       In Linux 2.4, if nnaannoosslleeeepp() is stopped by a  signal  (e.g.,  SSIIGGTTSSTTPP),
       then the call fails with the error EEIINNTTRR after the thread is resumed by
       a  SSIIGGCCOONNTT  signal.  If the system call is subsequently restarted, then
       the time that the thread spent in the  stopped  state  is  _n_o_t  counted
       against  the  sleep interval.  This problem is fixed in Linux 2.6.0 and
       later kernels.

